# Undefined variable: $categories in Laravel 5

When debugging a Blade template that suddenly throws an `Undefined variable` error, it is tempting to assume the controller failed to pass the data. However, looking closely at the code reveals a much subtler culprit: the scope of variables within a Blade layout.

In your specific case, the `CategoriesController` correctly populates the `$categories` collection and passes it via the `compact('categories')` method:

```php
public function index()
{
    $categories = Category::all();
    return view('/home', compact('categories'));
}
```

This logic is sound. The variable exists in the controller's scope and is explicitly attached to the view being rendered. When you switch to the `Articles` section, it likely works because `$articles` is also passed through `compact()`. If only one data set is missing from the `compact()` call, or if the layout structure is different, that single variable will vanish into thin air.

The root cause lies in how Laravel's view rendering system handles layouts and sections. Your `home.blade.php` file extends a master layout (likely named `app`). In Laravel 5, there is a crucial distinction between variables passed to the main view and those defined within the `@section` blocks.

By default, when you use `@extends('app')`, any variable available in the current controller's scope is automatically accessible within the entire layout hierarchy, including the `@section` content and the master layout itself. However, if your layout file (`resources/views/app.blade.php`) contains a conditional check or a specific rendering block that expects variables to be passed explicitly, or if you have inadvertently placed the loop outside a section where it should be, the variable might not resolve correctly depending on how `app.blade.php` is structured.

A common pattern that causes this exact issue involves the `@yield` directive. If your `app.blade.php` layout looks something like this:

```blade
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>...</head>
<body>
    <nav>...</nav>

    @yield('content') <!-- Variables passed to 'home' are available here -->
</body>
</html>
```

This works fine. However, if your `app.blade.php` attempts to access `$categories` or `$articles` directly outside of the `@yield` blockâperhaps to display a sidebar or a footer widgetâthe variables passed via `compact()` in the child view (`home`) are **not** automatically available there unless explicitly shared.

In your provided code snippet, you have nested logic:
1.  A sidebar section (inside `col-md-4`).
2.  The main content section (inside `col-md-8`).

If your master layout (`app.blade.php`) has a sidebar container that runs before the `@yield('content')` call, and that container tries to render categories or articles, those variables will be undefined because they haven't been pushed into the global context of the layout yet. They are only available inside the `@section('content')` block passed by the child view.

To fix this without restructuring your entire layout system, you have two primary options:

### Option 1: Move Logic Inside the Section
Ensure that any variable usage (like the `foreach` loop for categories) happens strictly within the `@section('content')` or `@yield('content')` blocks in your `home.blade.php`. If your layout expects variables to be available globally, you must pass them explicitly using `with()`:

```blade
@extends('app')

@section('content', function () {
    // Ensure $categories is accessible if the layout logic depends on it 
    // or if you need to share data with a parent section.
    return view('home-content', compact('categories')); 
})
```
*Note: This is complex and usually indicates a need to refactor.*

### Option 2: The Explicit `with` Method (Recommended)
The most robust solution in Laravel 5 when dealing with layouts that might share state across different sections is to explicitly share the data using the `with()` method. This forces the variable into the global scope of the layout, making it accessible even if your `app.blade.php` tries to read it outside the specific yield block.

Update your controller or the view directly:

**Controller Approach:**
```php
public function index()
{
    $categories = Category::all();
    $articles = Article::all(); // Ensure articles are also loaded if used in sidebar

    return view('home', compact('categories', 'articles'));
}
```

If you suspect the layout is sharing data incorrectly, try passing the variable explicitly in the view before the section starts:

```blade
@extends('app')

@push('stylesheets')
    <!-- Styles -->
@endpush

@section('content')
    @if(isset($categories))
        <div class="col-md-4 pull-right">
            <div class="panel panel-primary">
                <div class="panel-heading">
                    <h3 class="panel-title">Categories</h3>
                </div>
                <div class="panel-body">
                    @foreach($categories as $cat)
                        <!-- Your loop logic -->
                        <li><a href="articles/category/{{ $cat->slug }}">{{ $cat->name }}</a></li>
                    @endforeach
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    @endif

    @if(isset($articles))
        <!-- Existing articles logic -->
    @endif
@endsection
```

### Why Articles Worked but Categories Didn't
The discrepancy you observed ("Everything works fine with Articles but not categories") strongly suggests that `$articles` was passed via `compact()` in the controller, while `$categories` might have been missing from that compact list initially, or the layout structure changed between development of those two features.

If your layout (`app.blade.php`) has a sidebar component that renders *before* the `@yield('content')` call, and that sidebar attempts to display categories, you will hit the undefined variable error because `$categories` is scoped only to the main content section. Articles likely worked because they were either passed globally or their rendering was confined strictly inside the yield block where scope is inherited.

By ensuring both variables are present in `compact()` and verifying that your layout doesn't attempt to access them outside the intended sections, you eliminate the scope ambiguity that Laravel 5 enforces strictly for safety.
